// The inline path (consumer.deadLetter → Publisher.Publish) captures a delivery
// just before the consumer Terms it. A pod that dies in that window — OOM,
// SIGKILL, node eviction between "give up" and "publish to the DLQ" — drops the
// delivery UNCAPTURED, which is the exact non-reconstructible loss the DLQ exists
// to prevent (ingest already 200'd GitHub). This backstop closes that window: it
// listens for the JetStream advisories nats-server emits when a delivery finally
// exhausts MaxDeliver (MAX_DELIVERIES) or is explicitly Term'd (MSG_TERMINATED),
// fetches the original message out of webhooks_github_v1 by sequence, and
// captures it to the DLQ out-of-band.
//
// Idempotent against the inline path via the SAME Nats-Msg-Id dedup: both derive
// MsgID(source, deliveryID, envelope), so whichever path publishes a given
// delivery second lands as a server-side duplicate (ack.Duplicate) — counted as
// outcome=deduped and NOT re-counted on webhook.dlq.published, so the happy path
// (inline capture + advisory) collapses to one record, not two.
//
// One Backstop runs per consumer, IN that consumer's process, scoped to that
// consumer's durable and tagged with its source (fanout|webhook-forwarder|
// core-fanout). That
// scoping is load-bearing: it guarantees the backstop's source tag matches the
// inline capture's source, which is what makes the MsgID dedup line up. A single
// shared backstop handling every consumer's advisories would have to map
// consumer-name→source and a mismatch would silently double-capture; per-consumer
// embedding makes it foolproof and reuses each consumer's existing js + Publisher.
//
// webhooks_github_v1 is an INTEREST-retention stream (unlike mirror_git_v1's
// WorkQueue), so the fetchability of the original differs from the worker's:
//   - MAX_DELIVERIES fires when a delivery exhausts MaxDeliver still UNacked — the
//     pod-crash gap. The message is held by this consumer's unsatisfied interest,
//     so GetMsg succeeds and this is where genuine fresh captures come from.
//   - MSG_TERMINATED fires right after the consumer Terms (which already ran the
//     inline capture). Under interest retention the message survives iff the OTHER
//     consumer still has interest; if this was the last interest, Term removed it
//     and GetMsg misses (outcome=missing — benign, the inline path captured it).
//     When it IS still fetchable the backstop re-captures and dedups — except in
