      "text_snapshot": "Shared plumbing for Track D (COR-1254, implementing COR-1009 ph. 5). No consumer, no durable, no fleet file. Every later loop PR bases on this so each one carries a migration and nothing else.\nFour non-vendor files. The rest of the diff is vendor/.\nWhy a PR-0 at all\nThree changes that all twelve loop PRs would otherwise each carry, and conflict on:\n1. go-nuts v0.9.1-0.20260811083507 → v0.9.1-0.20260813130528-832d765dac1b. The old pin predates trail #13 by two days, so the vendored tree had no jsconsumer package at all and natsmsg was missing KeepInProgress's tick cap and the DLQ publisher's header policy. Nothing here imports jsconsumer yet — it vendors because internal/timingcontract does.\n2. internal/natstest — an embedded JetStream nats-server for tests. mirror-pipeline has had zero broker-level coverage: every consume-loop test drives interface fakes (ackableMsg, fanoutengine.AckableMsg, messageFetcher) that encode the same model as the code, so they agree with it exactly when it is wrong. That is the gap COR-1009 names as a gate for the loop ports, and the one go-nuts closed for itself with internal/brokersemantics after every P1 in the A1 review cycle turned out to be a model error rather than a coding one.\nIt exposes both handles on one broker — LegacyJetStream for the loops as they are today, JetStream for a migrated one — so a migration can be measured rather than asserted: characterise the legacy loop, then show the new one reproduces it. Two assertions written against two different fakes are not evidence of anything.\n3. internal/timingcontract — a consumer's redelivery timing as data, checked by go-nuts' exported jsconsumer.Schedule rather than by hand, plus the renderer for the before/after table every migration trail carries. ENT-1535 was a consumer whose real envelope was 34h22m against a config advertising 17h45m; duplicated timing arithmetic is how that happens, so nothing is restated here. Two loops in this repo — cmd/worker and the meta-fanout lifecycle fan — carry neither a server ladder nor a Retry, so jsconsumer.Start's own schedule check never runs for them, which is exactly why it has to be reachable from a test.\nPure by construction: no testing import, no NATS connection, no clock, so it carries none of natstest's production-import hazard.\nThe measurement worth reading\nREDACTED pins, against a real broker, the fact cmd/worker's client-ladder exception rests on — now recorded on COR-1254 as a class rule rather than a worker quirk:\nnats-server 2.14.4: requested AckWait 5s with BackOff[0] 200ms, stored AckWait 200ms\nA durable given a BackOff ladder does not keep its requested AckWait; the server stores BackOff[0] in its place. So \"own the ladder server-side\" and \"keep this AckWait\" are one knob, not two — and combined with natsmsg.KeepInProgress's ~5×AckWait cap, a 30s first rung would collapse the worker's extension budget from 75m to 2m30s and redeliver 30-minute clones mid-run. The fixture uses growing rungs deliberately: on a flat ladder the assertion passes whether the normalization happens or not.\nThe other two tests: TestVersionAttribution keeps a broker-semantic failure attributable to a server version, and TestNoProductionImport keeps the embedded server out of shipped binaries — guarding itself against passing vacuously by pinning that the walk actually reached a known consume loop, rather than trusting that a walk which found nothing means nothing is wrong.\nLoop 2's ladder, validated ahead of time\ninternal/timingcontract's renderer, run over the webhook-forwarder Shape A shape approved for loop 2. Both contracts pass jsconsumer.Schedule.Validate(); the envelope is preserved byte-for-byte:\n| delivery | before (client Nak) | after (server ladder) |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 1 | t+0s | t+0s |\n| 2 | t+30s | t+30s |\n| 3 | t+1m0s | t+1m0s |\n| 4 | t+1m30s | t+1m30s |\n| 5 | t+2m0s | t+2m0s |\n| 6 | t+2m30s | t+2m30s |\n| 7 | t+3m0s | t+3m0s |\n| 8 | t+3m30s — give up | t+3m30s — give up |\n| 9 |  | t+4m0s — capture reserve |\nThe blank 9th cell for the before contract is deliberate: the two schedules have different caps, and a 0s there would read as an instant retry.\nTwo judgment calls\nnats-server pinned to 2.14.4, not the 2.14.3 go-nuts resolves. The harness exists to attribute semantics to the server that actually runs, and fleet runs 2.14.4 (apps/nats/base/helmrelease.yaml). It is a test-only dependency, so no shipped binary changes — but it pulls x/sys, x/text and x/sync minor upgrades into the module, and those are shared with production code. Flagging rather than burying: a test-only dep moving shared libraries is worth a reviewer's eye.\nOne commit rather than three, against the usual grain. The vendor tree is coupled across all three parts (jsconsumer vendors only because part 3 imports it, nats-server only because part 2 does), so splitting would leave intermediate commits failing go mod tidy -diff. A bisect landing on a red commit costs more than one long commit message.\nPer-PR template\nBoth items from the binding template are N/A for this PR, stated rather than skipped:\n(a) stored-vs-rendered nats consumer info -j diff — this PR touches no durable and creates no consumer. The first PR that needs it is loop 1 (meta-fanout-lifecycle, one durable per cell). Note for planning: no MCP tool exposes consumer config — nats_stream_consumers returns runtime state only (ack floor, pending, redelivered, waiting) — so the snapshot has to come from someone with cluster credentials, per PR, until that gap is closed.\n(b) monitor/runbook text delta — no give-up semantics change here, so there is nothing for a responder to re-learn. Loop 2 is where that starts (capture-then-Ack silences the MAX_DELIVERIES/MSG_TERMINATED advisory backstops on the normal path).\nGate\ngo build ./..., go vet ./..., go mod tidy -diff, git diff --check, gofmt -l -s, go test ./..., go test -race ./..., mise run lint (golangci-lint 2.11.2 — 0 issue