se are one knob, so entire-search's 5m first rung would also slow post-crash redelivery 10×, and this handler is a single fast POST behind a one-message prefetch.\nThe \"before\" envelope is asserted too, so the snapshot cannot rot silently: if someone reconciles that durable, the test goes red and names the date the measurement was taken.\nTwo judgment calls\nnats-server pinned to 2.14.4, not the 2.14.3 go-nuts resolves. The harness exists to attribute semantics to the server that actually runs, and fleet runs 2.14.4 (apps/nats/base/helmrelease.yaml). Test-only, so no shipped binary changes — but it pulls x/sys, x/text and x/sync minor upgrades into the module, and those are shared with production code.\nOne commit for the plumbing rather than three. The vendor tree is coupled across all three parts (jsconsumer vendors only because part 3 imports it, nats-server only because part 2 does), so splitting would leave intermediate commits failing go mod tidy -diff. The second commit is the fixture correction above, kept separate because it is a finding rather than plumbing.\nPer-PR template\n(a) stored-vs-rendered nats consumer info -j diff — N/A: this PR touches no durable and creates no consumer. Loop 1's pre-flight is nevertheless complete, and clean: meta-fanout-lifecycle is identical across all seven cells (4 prod + 3 staging) — ackWait 30s, maxDeliver 8, no backoff, no inactiveThreshold, maxAckPending 65536, deliverPolicy all, replayPolicy instant, ackPolicy explicit, maxWaiting 512, numReplicas 0. The one field a migration would move unintentionally is max_ack_pending: every durable in every cell carries 65536, which is neither the server's 1000 default nor a tuned value — nats.go's legacy pull path copies its own subscription-channel capacity into the server config (js.go:1879-1880, cfg.MaxAckPending = cap(ch)). Leaving MaxAckPending at zero would silently rewrite it to 1000, so each loop states it explicitly.\n(b) monitor/runbook text delta — N/A: no give-up semantics change here. Loop 2 is where that starts (capture-then-Ack silences the MAX_DELIVERIES/MSG_TERMINATED advisory backstops on the normal path), and COR-1270 carries the ack-floor monitor that stream lacks entirely.\nGate\ngo build ./..., go vet ./..., go mod tidy -diff, git diff --check, gofmt -l -s, go test ./..., go test -race ./..., mise run lint (golangci-lint 2.11.2 — 0 issues). All clean.\nReviewing this\nThe vendor diff is ~205k lines and carries no information; read the four files under internal/natstest/ and internal/timingcontract/, plus go.mod. Worth pressing on: whether the 2.14.4 pin is right, and whether TestNoProductionImport's self-guards earn their keep.",
