      "text_snapshot": "Loop 1 of Track D (COR-1254, implementing COR-1009 ph. 5). The cross-region resource_lifecycle_v1 fan moves off its hand-rolled PullSubscribe/Fetch(1) loop onto jsconsumer.Run + Process, and its redelivery ladder moves server-side. Same durable name — no recreation, no redelivery storm.\nChosen as loop 1 because it is the smallest self-contained migration in the plan: one durable, low volume, a limits-retention stream where nothing pins, and no give-up decision to make. It proves the scaffold and the server-ladder pattern with nothing else moving.\nThree commits: the merged-PR-0 follow-up, the migration, the tests. The first is unrelated to the migration and reviews separately.\nTiming contract\nGenerated by internal/timingcontract from the shipped constants and validated by jsconsumer.Schedule, not typed by hand:\n| delivery | before (client Nak, measured) | after (server ladder) |\n|---:|---|---|\n| 1 | t+0s | t+0s |\n| 2 | t+30s | t+30s |\n| 3 | t+1m0s | t+1m0s |\n| 4 | t+1m30s | t+1m30s |\n| 5 | t+2m0s | t+2m0s |\n| 6 | t+2m30s | t+2m30s |\n| 7 | t+3m0s | t+3m0s |\n| 8 | t+3m30s — give up | t+3m30s — give up |\nThe envelope does not move; only ownership of the schedule does. The pre-flight is what licenses that claim rather than assuming it — see below.\nPre-flight: stored vs rendered, all seven cells\nnatsctl -- consumer info resource_lifecycle_v1 meta-fanout-lifecycle --json, on 4 prod cells (us-east-2, eu-central-1, ap-south-1, ap-southeast-2) and 3 staging (us-west-2, us-east-2, eu-west-1). All seven identical:\n| field | stored today | rendered by this PR | delta | |---|---|---|---| | ack_wait | 30s | 30s | — | | max_deliver | 8 | 8 | — | | ack_policy | explicit | explicit | — | | deliver_policy | all | all | — | | replay_policy | instant | instant | — | | filter_subject | resource_lifecycle_v1.\u003e | same | — | | max_waiting | 512 | 512 (server default) | — | | num_replicas | 0 (inherits stream) | 0 | — | | inactive_threshold | absent | absent, now explicit | — | | backoff | absent | [30s × 7] | intended | | max_ack_pending | 65536 | 1000 | intended, see below |\nTwo intended deltas, nothing else. Both are mutable, so this is an update on the existing durable rather than a recreate.\nWorth stating explicitly: this durable has no undeclared server ladder, which is why loop 1 can claim envelope preservation. webhook-forwarder and fanout-\u003cregion\u003e are not like this — they carry an undeclared 41h12m30s ladder in prod (COR-1270), so loop 2 will not be able to make the same claim.\nThe MaxAckPending change\n65536 was never chosen by anyone: nats.go's legacy pull path copies its own client-side subscription-channel capacity into the server config (js.go, cfg.MaxAckPending = cap(ch)). jsconsumer sends the field on every start, so leaving it zero would have rewritten all seven durables to the server default silently. Setting 1000 makes it a recorded decision instead. Either number is generous at a one-message prefetch — in-flight work is one delivery per replica.\nWhat does not move\nGive-up semantics. No Term path, no Retry, no DLQ. The event is republished verbatim, so nothing about it can be permanently unprocessable; and resource_lifecycle_v1 is limits-retention, holding every event for its full max age whatever this consumer does — so the replay path is the source stream, not a capture stream. Adding capture would need a DLQ stream and a meta-fanout publish grant, neither of which exists; out of scope by rule.\nInactiveThreshold stays unset, and now says so in code. The 2026-06-01 reaping valve exists for interest streams, where an abandoned durable pins every subsequent delivery for ALL consumers until the stream's maxAge. On limits retention an idle durable pins nothing and reaping it would only cost a cursor.\nTwo known deltas\n/readyz is narrower. The old loop held a subscribed bool set after each successful PullSubscribe, so a retryable boot failure (stream not provisioned yet, no responders) also read NotReady. jsconsumer.Run exposes no start-success signal, so Ready() now reports only that the configuration was accepted — which does cover this migration's likeliest failure, an immutable-field disagreement against the live consumer. In exchange, Run recreates a consume loop that closes mid-run, as the old outer loop did. Closing the gap needs go-nuts to expose readiness from Run; recorded beside the Runner.Drain and FloorMonitor-attach gaps entire-search #131 already logged.\nThe fan-failure log drops redeliver_in, gains max_deliver. The app no longer schedules the redelivery, so printing a delay would be a claim about someone else's config — true only while the two happen to agree, which is the drift this whole track exists to remove. Position in the ladder is what a responder can act on; the ladder itself is on the durable.\noutcome=nak is kept verbatim on the metric — dashboards key on it and its meaning is unchanged (this delivery failed and will come back). What changed is who schedules the redelivery, which the metric never described.\nReal-broker coverage\nGive-up semantics changed, so this needs broker evidence rather than fake evidence. The existing unit tests answer what the handler does; these answer what the broker does about it, which fakes cannot — they encode the same model as the code, so a belief held wrongly in both places is green.\nStoredConfigIsTheRenderedConfig — the executable form of the table above: the real config through the real CreateOrUpdateConsumer, read back from the server.\nFanFailure_IsRedeliveredByTheServerLadder — settle nothing, get the delivery back on the ladder. The fixture ladder is scaled to milliseconds and growing on purpose: on a flat ladder BackOff[0] equals AckWait, so the test would pass whether redelivery followed the ladder or merely the ack timeout — pinning nothing. Gap tolerances are half the distance to the nearest rival explanation (~0 for a plain Nak, rung1 for a ladder that does not advance).\nCleanFan_AdvancesTheAckFloor — the success path on the ack floor itself, the signal every stall alert reads. FakeMsg can only show Ack was called; a denied $JS.ACK publish returns no error at all (COR-1224), so \"we called Ack\" and \"the message is settled\" are different claims.\nTwo fixture problems found and fixed while writing these, both worth knowing: recordingJS records only successful publishes so its count reads zero however often the fan was attempted (a local failingJS counts attempts); and publishing the arrival timestamp before the handler ran let the test assert a count that had not happened yet. Stable across -count=3 and under -race.\nCommit 1 is a PR-0 follow-up\nLate Bugbot finding on the merged #245: natstest.Server registered t.Cleanup(s.Shutdown) only after waiting for readiness, so a readiness timeout hit t.Fatalf with the server still bound to its port and holding the JetStream store — and because t.TempDir registered its cleanup earlier and cleanups run LIFO, the store directory would be removed underneath a live server. The symptom lands in a later parallel test. Cleanup is now registered before the wait.\nCarried here because #245 has merged and loop 1 is the harness's first real consumer; kept as its own commit. No regression test: forcing a readiness timeout needs a hook that does not exist, and an assertion that only covered the happy path would pin nothing.\nPer-PR template\n(a) stored-vs-rendered diff — the table above, seven cells, plus StoredConfigIsTheRenderedConfig as its executable form.\n(b) monitor/runbook delta — none needed. Give-up semantics are unchanged (no new terminal state, no DLQ, same 8-delivery envelope), so there is nothing new for a responder to learn. The metric vocabulary is unchanged. The one operator-visible change is the log field swap described above, which no runbook or monitor references.\nGate\ngo build ./..., go vet ./..., go mod tidy -diff, git diff --check, gofmt -l -s, go test ./..., go test -race, mise run lint (golangci-lint 2.11.2 — 0 issues). All clean.\nDeploying this\nmeta-fanout runs in every cell, and in lifecycle-only mode in the non-US cells — where this consumer is the /readyz gate. Worth watching on rollout: pod readiness in a lifecycle-only cell, meta_fanout.lifecycle_fanned{outcome} staying on ack, and the durable's backoff appearing in nats consumer info with max_ack_pending at 1000.",
